 Group Report 
1. Introduction

This project was carried out over three sprints to design and implement a software system using software architectural styles and design principles. The goal was to move systematically from requirements to a working system while applying key concepts such as components, connectors, modularity, and architectural structure.

The project emphasised the use of architectural styles, which define how components interact via connectors and follow specific configuration rules and semantics . This ensured the system is scalable, maintainable, and well-structured.

2. Sprint 1 – Requirements and Setup

During Sprint 1, we identified both functional and non-functional requirements, defining the system scope and expected behaviour.

We also:

Set up a development environment using GitHub
Organised work using project management tools
Established a Code of Conduct for effective collaboration

From an architectural perspective:

An initial UML component diagram was created to represent the system using components and connectors
Components were defined with provided and required interfaces
Basic architectural elements were implemented in Java using interfaces and abstract classes, reflecting the concept of component-based design

This stage laid the foundation for a structured architecture where components communicate through clearly defined interfaces.

3. Sprint 2 – Architecture Design

In Sprint 2, we refined the system using formal architectural models:

Models Created
Business Concept Model → captures domain concepts
Use Case Diagram → shows user interactions
System Interfaces → define operations between components
Business Type Model → structures domain entities

These models were integrated into a system architecture that describes:

Components (functional units)
Connectors (interaction mechanisms between components)

We applied Clean Architecture, which aligns with the layered architectural style, where:

Each layer provides services to the layer above
Dependencies are controlled and structured

This separation of concerns improved:

Maintainability
Reusability
Testability
4. Sprint 3 – Implementation and Testing

In Sprint 3, the system was extended and implemented using more advanced architectural concepts from the slides.

 Compound Components

The system was structured into compound components, meaning:

Each high-level component contains internal sub-components
External interfaces delegate requests to internal components using delegation connectors

This improves:

Encapsulation
Modularity
Reusability
 Architectural Styles Applied

We applied multiple domain-independent architectural styles:

Model-View-Controller (MVC)
Model → manages data and domain logic
View → handles user interface
Controller → manages user interaction
Layered Architecture
System divided into hierarchical layers
Each layer communicates via defined interfaces
Service-Oriented Architecture (SOA)
Components behave as services
Communication is loosely coupled via connectors
Improves interoperability and scalability
 Connectors and Communication
Components interact through connectors
Both assembly connectors (between components) and delegation connectors (within compound components) were used
Communication semantics such as synchronous interaction were considered using UML stereotypes
 Testing

The system was fully implemented and tested using defined test cases. Results confirmed:

Correct functionality
Proper interaction between components
Compliance with requirements
